// validateUpstreamUserinfoEscape rejects an http or https upstream whose
// authority carries userinfo with a percent escape that is not well formed:
//
//	scheme "://" username [":" password] "@" host [basePath]
//
// A "%" in the username or password must be followed by two hexadecimal
// digits, exactly as elsewhere in the address; a literal percent is written
// "%25". url.Parse reports such an escape as an EscapeError whose text
// repeats the offending bytes — invalid URL escape "%zz" for the password
// "%zz" — and those bytes are part of the credential, so letting the
// generic parse error through would re-leak the secret in the reason even
// after the address itself is shown with the userinfo replaced by "***".
// The check therefore runs on the raw splitUpstream boundaries before
// url.Parse: attribution follows parseAuthority's own host-before-userinfo
// order (the host is parsed on its own first), so an invalid escape that
// actually belongs to the host keeps the generic diagnostic with its
// fragment intact, and a later base-path escape can never be blamed on the
// userinfo — an '@' inside the base path is not an authority boundary.
// Only http and https upstreams with userinfo are in scope; other schemes
// and addresses without userinfo keep their existing diagnostics.
//
// On failure the reason names the route's 1-based position, its id and the
// upstream field, shows the address with the whole username and optional
// password replaced by "***" (the '@' and the rest of the address kept),
// and never repeats the offending fragment or any other credential byte.
// Like the other field-content rules this is check layer 4
// (Config.validate), in routes-array order, so the whole configuration is
// rejected even when no request could hit the offending route and no config
// is returned.
